.TH EXECAC 2
.SH NAME
execac \- execute a file on an application core
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.PP
.B
int execac(int core, char *name, char* argv[])
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Execac
is
.IR exec (2),
after which the process runs on the application core
.IR core ,
on any free one if
.I core
is negative, or on the time-sharing cores if
.I core
is zero.
.PP
Application cores (ACs) are the processors the boot option
.B *nixac
(see
.IR plan9.ini (8))
sets aside.
An AC runs no scheduler and takes no clock and no device interrupts;
it runs the user code of one process at a time, undisturbed.
The process' system calls, page faults and notes are served by
the kernel on a time-sharing core (TC), which then sends the
process back to the AC.
The calls work as usual but take a round trip between the cores;
the process should compute rather than call.
Its children, made by
.IR fork (2),
run on the TCs.
The
.B core
file and the
.B core
control message of
.IR proc (3)
show and change the core of a process.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/port/nix.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR execac (1),
.IR exec (2),
.IR proc (3),
.IR plan9.ini (8)
.br
.I
NIX: a case for a manycore system for cloud computing,
Bell Labs Technical Journal, 2012.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Sets
.IR errstr :
.B no application cores
(none at boot, and always on 386),
.BR "not an application core" ,
.BR "application core busy" ,
.BR "no free application core" .
.SH BUGS
An AC is reserved for one process; its other cores stay idle.
